# Fixed: "Issac" -> "Isaac" (7067, 7069). Nothing else.

=== Introduction
--- Genesis Chapter 21 [title]
overline: Young Adult Sunday School • Genesis Series
heading: Genesis 21: The Bondwoman and the Freewoman
sub: Isaac Born • Cast Out the Bondwoman • Liberty in Christ • Hagar's Well • Beersheba
refs: Genesis 21:1-34
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

=== The son of the promise
--- The LORD Visited Sarah
kicker: vv. 1–3
sub: At the Set Time
scripture: Genesis 21:1-3 | mark: at the set time of which God had spoken to him
p: Now Abraham had been sojourning in King Abimelech's land of Gerar for a while, long enough for Sarah to conceive and bear Isaac, and for him to be weaned. The birth of the son by faith, as promised to Abraham by God.

--- An Hundred Years Old
kicker: vv. 4–5
sub: Eight Days Old
scripture: Genesis 21:4-5
p: Abraham was 100, meaning Sarah was 90.

--- God Hath Made Me to Laugh
kicker: vv. 6–7
sub: Who Would Have Said unto Abraham?
scripture: Genesis 21:6-7 | mark: God hath made me to laugh
p: If we remember back a few chapters, upon hearing the promise directly from the Lord, Sarah laughed at the idea. Who would believe it, in other words. It's a funny situation, she thinks others will share in the humor of it.

=== Cast out the bondwoman
--- Mocking
kicker: vv. 8–10
sub: Cast Out This Bondwoman and Her Son
scripture: Genesis 21:8-10 | mark: mocking; Cast out this bondwoman and her son
p: But the son of the bondwoman mocked the situation. Ishmael laughed at it.

--- In Isaac Shall Thy Seed Be Called
kicker: vv. 11–13
sub: Hearken unto Her Voice
scripture: Genesis 21:11-13 | mark: for in Isaac shall thy seed be called
p: This dichotomy always struck me as more about Sarah and her own self-consciousness and regret of the sin she foisted on Abraham with Hagar. But she insisted they be cast out, and God was in agreement with her.
p: So with the blessing and consolation of the Lord, Abraham cast out the son of the bondwoman. A picture of the salvation to come in faith, through Jesus, Abraham's seed of the promise, and a rejection of being bond to the works of the law.

--- Which Things Are an Allegory
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 21:10
fig: Galatians 4:22-24 | Galatians 4:22-24
fig: Galatians 4:30-31 | Galatians 4:30-31
callout: Again, the New Testament is the study guide for the Old. This event, the casting out of Hagar and Ishmael, the father of Arabia is a picture of the covenant of Mt Sinai, the law of Moses. But the covenant of Jerusalem is free. Liberty from the law in Christ. [[Galatians 4:21-31|Read Galatians 4:21–31]]

--- The Bondage of the Law
kicker: Application • Genesis 21:10
sub: Return to the Bondage of the Law
p: Many seek to return to the bondage of the law, even in Paul's day. Paul questions the salvation of the foolish Galatians who were adding works to the Gospel of reconciliation through faith, just as we do today, when someone professing themselves to be Christians add works of the law to be saved. Be they turning from sins which is obedience to the law, requiring circumcision, baptism, rites and rituals, denominational subservience, paying alms, fasting of meats...any act of the flesh designed to give righteousness to the man added to the imputed righteousness of Christ Jesus. Return to the bondage of the law, *by which no flesh be justified.*
p: If you are trusting in your works to save you, help save you, or keep your salvation, you are perishing, in your bonds.

--- The Children of the Promise
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 21:12
p: This lesson, from Genesis and taught throughout the New Testament, made very clear here in Galatians, that the children of the promise live by faith, just as Isaac was the child of the promise of God, received in faith by Abraham.
fig: Romans 4:3-5 | Romans 4:3-5

--- Sacraments
kicker: Application • The children of the promise
sub: The Same Damnable Heresy
p: False churches like the Roman Catholics, adding adherence to their "sacraments," the vain traditions of men of rites and rituals they claim in their catechism, are required for salvation. That they dispense God's abundant grace which is given freely from the Covenant made in Jesus on the mount in Jerusalem, by works of the law. By bondage to themselves. It's the same damnable heresy being talked about in Galatians. Be it circumcision or infant "baptism."
p: Baptism of believers is an outward sign of their saving faith. Not a requirement for righteousness, but a way churches can know someone professes Christ as their salvation. But the wicked sprinkling or dunking of infants, like the command under the law to circumcise on the 8th day, is explicitly taught against as judaizing; adding works to salvation.

--- Not in Circumcision
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 21:4
p: It's a false gospel. How do we know? Because Abraham was saved when he called upon the name of the Lord. In circumcision? No, decades before that OT requirement was made.
fig: Romans 4:10-11 | Romans 4:10-11
p: Pay close attention to what Paul writes here. If you are counting on a rite or ritual, such as circumcision or baptism to be saved, you are making yourself heir to the law, faith is made void and the promises made of none effect. [[Romans 4:13-14]]
p: You're not saved. Repent.

--- Christ Shall Profit You Nothing
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 21:4
fig: Galatians 5:2-4 | Galatians 5:2-4
callout: The leaven of the Pharisees is the whole lump of Catholicism. Adding baptism or confession, communion, confirmation, last rites...adding any of it to the Gospel of salvation in faith through Christ Jesus makes you a debtor to the whole of the law. **Christ shall profit you nothing.** [[Galatians 5:2-9|Read Galatians 5:2–9]]

--- Stand Fast in the Liberty
kicker: Application • Liberty in Christ
sub: The Error of Cain
p: What the whore of Babylon, the Roman Catholic Church and its prot harlot daughters do is deny the liberty to the law that is in Christ Jesus, so that its adherents stay yoked to trying to justify themselves before God in their works.
p: But to we who are free in Christ by the free gift of salvation, we willingly become servants for righteousness, sanctifying ourselves, denying the flesh and walking in the spirit.
p: By denying the liberty, they damn those following their perverse false doctrine. And by relying on their vain works, they receive nothing but death and Hell, being debtors to the whole of the law.
fig: Galatians 5:1 | Galatians 5:1

--- Wood, Hay and Stubble
kicker: Application • Liberty in Christ
p: God's word describes the man who seeks to be justified by dead works and adherence to the law as under bondage. Satan tells us that our feeble attempts at our own righteousness is sufficient. The error of Cain. But God says our righteousness are as filthy rags. Wood, hay and stubble.
p: And just as He rejected Cain's works and accepted Abel's offering of faith, so too it is for us today.

--- No Requirement Other Than Faith
kicker: Application • The whole of scripture
p: It is important to note that the whole of scripture teaches the same thing; the free gift of eternal life given by God the Father in His grace, by faith in the blood of His Son, Christ Jesus, sealed by the Holy Spirit unto redemption.
p: There is no scripture that contradicts that Gospel. There is no secret to salvation, the knowledge is freely given. There is no requirement other than faith. All men are called, and know the wrath of God and His law, written in their hearts, so they have no excuse. But so many choose to be heirs of the bondwoman. Debtors to the law. And end up perishing.
p: These who self-righteously toil in the law, seek to put others in the same chains. They make entire books of doctrines and dogmas, created by man, but ignore scripture and the doctrines of God. It's tragic.

=== Hagar's well
--- The Water Was Spent
kicker: vv. 14–16
sub: The Wilderness of Beersheba
scripture: Genesis 21:14-16 | mark: And the water was spent in the bottle
p: Like Hagar and Ishmael, they wander in the wilderness. Their water, given by man, by Abraham, runs out. They thirst.

--- God Heard the Voice of the Lad
kicker: vv. 17–19
sub: She Saw a Well of Water
scripture: Genesis 21:17-19 | mark: And God heard the voice of the lad; And God opened her eyes, and she saw a well of water
p: But, just as Christ is the living water who satisfies our thirst to be sanctified by God, that we may never thirst, God provides a well that they may drink. Hagar desperately cries out to God. And He in His grace hears her;

--- God Was with the Lad
kicker: vv. 20–21
sub: The Wilderness of Paran
scripture: Genesis 21:20-21 | mark: And God was with the lad
p: This is a picture of salvation. Of Jesus. Even to the bondwoman, there is the offer of salvation by faith. Calling on the name of the Lord.

=== Beersheba
--- God Is with Thee
kicker: vv. 22–24
sub: Swear unto Me Here by God
scripture: Genesis 21:22-24 | mark: God is with thee in all that thou doest
p: Now Abimelech, seeing how God has blessed Abraham, making him mighty and rich, seeks to make a treaty with him.

--- A Well of Water
kicker: vv. 25–27
sub: Both of Them Made a Covenant
scripture: Genesis 21:25-27 | mark: both of them made a covenant

--- Seven Ewe Lambs
kicker: vv. 28–32
sub: Beersheba
scripture: Genesis 21:28-32 | mark: Wherefore he called that place Beersheba
p: For lambs and a well, in the place *Beersheba*, where their covenant was made. Abimelech swearing not only his own covenant, but those of his sons, and their sons. To the 4th generation.

--- The Everlasting God
kicker: vv. 33–34
sub: In the Philistines' Land
scripture: Genesis 21:33-34 | mark: called there on the name of the LORD, the everlasting God
p: And again, Abraham called upon the name of the Lord, to bless the place he sojourned.
p: The land of the Philistines, where 400+ years later, where the Hebrews, the sons of Abraham would find conflict with the sons of Abimelech.

--- Liberty in Jesus
center: Repent towards the good news of liberty in Jesus.
